  "query_preview": "..\n\nRapid thunderstorm development is ongoing across the northern Plains \nearly this morning along the nose of a strengthening southerly LLJ. \nThis activity is expected to organize into an MCS and progress \nthrough the Upper Midwest today before traversing into the Great \nLakes tonight into early tomorrow. Given little to no instability \nacross the Great Lakes, a weakening trend of this complex remains \nfavored, although latest hi-res guidance brings some increasing \nconfidence that portions of the MCS can reach Southeast Michigan. \n\nThis southerly low-level flow across the Plains will fold from Iowa \ninto Wisconsin through the morning hours and while the \naforementioned MCS is expected to outpace the nascent moisture \nsource, the nocturnal reinforcement of the LLJ could support \nredevelopment or reinvigoration of showers and embedded \nthunderstorms along the backside of the MCS despite limited \ninstability. This can support rain and thunderstorm chances into the \nearly afternoon across SE MI. Additionally this corridor of enhanced \ntheta-a will strengthen isentropic ascent along or north of the I-69 \ncorridor, providing continued support for showers and thunderstorms \ninto the latter half of the day. Development along this convergent \nboundary in the afternoon/evening would likely be a little more \nlocalized. Overall there remains uncertainty regarding how these \nindividual potential rounds of precipitation will evolve and \noverlap, but confidence has increased in a considerably more \nunsettled Sunday. \n\nGiven the above, confidence is increasing that NBM temperatures are \nlikely overdone on Sunday. An active convective pattern and \npersistent cloud cover will limit meaningful insolation. If the more \nactive setup plays out as projected, highs will be capped in the \n70s, with low 80s possible only where coverage is reduced for a \nlonger period of time. \n\nA very moist and active pattern will persist in the early and \nmidweek period. Ensemble guidance highlights strong west to east \nintegrated water vapor transport across the region into Wednesday. \nPW values of 1.75 to 2.00\" and warm-cloud depths approaching 13kft \nwill support efficient rainfall production. Mid-level disturbances \nwithin quasi-zonal flow under this setup will bring multiple \nopportunities for showers and thunderstorms. Repeated convection \nalong the same areas will bring a localized flooding risk. In the \nsame regard for Sunday temperatures, early week highs will be \nconditional on cloud coverage and shower chances. Temperatures will \nlikely need to be tempered down if new guidance highlights SE MI as \na susceptible corridor for repeated precipitation.",
